    {
      "id": "story-frame-2",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": 1800,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 1600,
      "height": 900,
      "name": "Slide 02",
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#202020",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "locked": false,
      "seed": 102,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 654676336,
      "customData": {
        "title": "The problem",
        "spoken": "Imagine buying a concert ticket as a QR code. You could send a screenshot to a friend, and the code might still scan. So the code alone doesn't prove who the person is. One solution is to keep a database of people's faces, but that creates another problem: face information is much harder to replace if it is stolen. I wanted the benefits of a face check without putting everyone's reusable face information in one place.",
        "keyPoints": "1. A QR code can be shared.\n2. A central face database creates a different risk.\n3. My idea tries to avoid both problems.",
        "originalSlideText": "PROBLEM\nA QR code proves you have a ticket — not who you are.\nPrivate Pass  /  MANAV CODATY\n02\nQR TICKET\nCan be shared\nA screenshot might work for someone else.\nFACE DATABASE\nStores lasting data\nA central set of face records creates new risk.\nPrivate Pass\nLinks ticket + person\nCheck the holder without a central face database.",
        "sources": [
          "/Users/manavcodaty/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Praco Family/EPQ/Mav/PrivatePass_EPQ Presentation_v4.pptx — slide 2"
        ],
        "claim": "The problem"
      },
      "index": "aG",
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793173192618,
      "link": null
    },
