      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "locked": false,
      "seed": 109,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 739180432,
      "customData": {
        "title": "Why the pass has two protections",
        "spoken": "I used two different ways to protect a pass. A digital signature works a bit like a stamp. It lets the gate check that the pass came from the right system and hasn't been changed. Encryption is more like putting the face information in a locked box, so only the prepared gate can read it. Those checks help protect the ticket, but they don't prove who is holding it. That's why the gate still needs to check the person's face.",
        "keyPoints": "1. Signature: genuine ticket, not changed.\n2. Encryption: keep face information private.\n3. Still need to match the person.",
        "originalSlideText": "PASS SECURITY\nSigned and encrypted are not the same.\nPrivate Pass  /  MANAV CODATY\n09\nDIGITAL SIGNATURE\nIs the pass genuine?\nShows who issued it and whether it was changed.\nENCRYPTION\nWho can read the face data?\nKeeps the face record protected until the prepared gate opens it.\nA VALID PASS STILL NEEDS A MATCHING PERSON",
        "sources": [
          "/Users/manavcodaty/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Praco Family/EPQ/Mav/PrivatePass_EPQ Presentation_v4.pptx — slide 9"
        ],
        "claim": "Why the pass has two protections"
      },
      "index": "b13",
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793173192618,
      "link": null
    },
